1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:03,895
This is Ghost Bills. Here is a three minute tour.

2
00:00:04,045 --> 00:00:10,446
Ghost Bills reads your bank statement and finds the subscriptions you forgot about. We call them ghosts.

3
00:00:10,596 --> 00:00:18,235
Why did we make it? Small subscriptions are easy to forget. A few dollars a month can add up to hundreds of dollars a year.

4
00:00:18,385 --> 00:00:22,781
Many apps that find them want your bank login. Ghost Bills does not.

5
00:00:22,931 --> 00:00:28,458
Everything runs on your own laptop, even the AI. Your bank data never leaves it.

6
00:00:28,608 --> 00:00:36,183
To start, install Ollama and download the Gemma model. Then run one command, and open the app in your browser.

7
00:00:36,333 --> 00:00:42,649
Penny, the black cat, is your guide. The app comes with a sample statement, so you can try it right away.

8
00:00:42,799 --> 00:00:48,710
Step one. Download a CSV file from your bank's website. Then click Import statement.

9
00:00:49,210 --> 00:00:53,435
Ghost Bills finds the date, description and amount columns for you.

10
00:00:53,585 --> 00:00:58,877
Check the first rows, then click Import. If your dates are unclear, Penny will ask you.

11
00:00:59,377 --> 00:01:05,373
Step two. Ghost Bills looks for charges that repeat, and checks them with four simple rules.

12
00:01:05,523 --> 00:01:12,906
A Poltergeist is a price that went up. Moonbeam Music went from $9.99 to $12.99 a month.

13
00:01:13,056 --> 00:01:21,185
A Sleepy Ghost is a yearly plan that will charge you soon. CloudCrate will take $119.00 in 9 days.

14
00:01:21,335 --> 00:01:25,198
A Shapeshifter is a free trial that turned into a paid plan.

15
00:01:25,348 --> 00:01:30,235
Twin Ghosts are two subscriptions that do the same job, like two music apps.

16
00:01:30,385 --> 00:01:38,322
At the top, you see what all your ghosts cost you each year. Here, it is $466.76.

17
00:01:38,472 --> 00:01:44,233
Normal shopping, rent, and tiny price changes are not flagged. The rules are careful.

18
00:01:44,383 --> 00:01:46,987
Step three. Click a ghost to see the proof.

19
00:01:47,487 --> 00:01:54,443
These numbers come straight from your own statement. Plain code finds them, not AI, so you can check every one.

20
00:01:54,593 --> 00:02:03,682
Below, Gemma explains why it matters, in simple words. Gemma is an open AI model that runs on your laptop, inside Ollama.

21
00:02:03,832 --> 00:02:12,985
Gemma never does the math. A checker blocks any number, date or link that is not in the proof. If Gemma is off, you get simple template text.

22
00:02:13,135 --> 00:02:18,043
Step four. When Ghost Bills is not sure, Penny asks you, instead of guessing.

23
00:02:18,193 --> 00:02:26,728
Here, a VPN price went up a little. It is billed in another currency, so it might just be the exchange rate. You choose the answer.

24
00:02:27,228 --> 00:02:30,515
Penny remembers your answer, so she never asks twice.

25
00:02:30,665 --> 00:02:36,490
Step five. You decide what to cancel. Ghost Bills writes a short cancel email for you.

26
00:02:36,640 --> 00:02:46,220
Click Copy email, then send it to the company yourself, or use the cancel option in your account settings. Ghost Bills never sends anything for you.

27
00:02:46,720 --> 00:02:49,431
When you have cancelled it, click I cancelled it.

28
00:02:49,931 --> 00:02:55,778
The ghost moves to the graveyard, and Saved so far goes up. Changed your mind? Click Undo.

29
00:02:55,928 --> 00:03:01,028
Upcoming charges shows what will be taken from your account in the next 30 days.

30
00:03:01,178 --> 00:03:09,862
You can also ask Penny questions, like how much do I spend on music? Penny looks it up with read-only tools, and code does the math.

31
00:03:10,012 --> 00:03:12,701
And just for fun, click Start the séance.

32
00:03:13,201 --> 00:03:17,533
Penny shows your ghosts one by one, biggest first, by candlelight.

33
00:03:17,683 --> 00:03:22,890
That is Ghost Bills. Code finds the ghosts. AI explains them. You decide.

34
00:03:23,040 --> 00:03:26,497
Your bank data stays on your laptop. Happy haunting!
